AB  - Cloud computing is increasingly becoming an outsourced platform of storing data and computing, but the issue of privacy arises with large concern when sensitive data is stored on clouds. Homomorphic Encryption (HE) has become one of the potential cryptographic methods that promote the opportunity to make computations on encrypted records that allow keeping sensitive data secret during the processing phase. This feature enables cloud computing vendors to perform operations on client data without having to store it in its plaintexts, thus maintaining privacy of data. This article provides an in-depth analysis of the homomorphic encryption methods on privacy preserving cloud computing. It discusses the core concepts of HE and evaluates various schemes, which are partially homomorphic encryption (PHE), somewhat homomorphic encryption (SHE) and fully homomorphic encryption (FHE). The work also explores the mathematical basis, security assurances as well as the trade-offs of performance of these encryption schemes. Along with that, recent development efforts to minimize the computation time and enhance the scale are also described. This entire work is novel as it offers combined analysis of HE schemes with regard to practical applications of cloud-based solutions, such as encrypted query of databases, secure machine learning, and privacy-sensitive medical data analytics. The tradeoff point on the security, computation cost, and scalability of real-world deployments is indicated in the analysis. Despite the fact that homomorphic encryption has proven to possess a remarkable potential of facilitating secure cloud services, there are still challenges associated with its efficiency and the ability to employed it in large scale applications. Further sophistications of cryptography algorithms and computation equipment is bound to increase the feasibility of HE-based privacy-preserving cloud systems even more.
