AB  - Homomorphic Encryption (HE) enables computation on encrypted data, offering a promising solution for privacy-preserving applications such as identity verification and medical image analysis. The CKKS scheme, in particular, is well-suited for convolutional neural network (CNN) inference due to its support for realnumber arithmetic. However, HE-based CNN inference suffers from high computational latency and resource overhead, hindering its practical implementation. In addition, existing acceleration methods are mainly designed for single-image or large-batch processing, lacking a solution for efficiently processing a moderate number of input images with fast homomorphic inference capabilities. To address this, we introduce a novel leveled homomorphic CNN inference scheme aimed at reducing latency and improving throughput using the CKKS scheme. Our approach includes a novel batch packing method that maps multiple inputs into ciphertexts by leveraging convolutional sliding windows and SIMD capabilities. We further introduce optimizations such as mask-weight merging, rotation multiplexing, stride convolution segmentation, and folding rotations to minimize computational cost. Evaluations on MNIST and CIFAR-10 demonstrate that our method completes inference for 163 MNIST images in 10.4 seconds on a single CPU thread with an accuracy of 98.95%, achieving a 6.9× throughput improvement over state-of-the-art works, while also reducing communication and memory overhead.
ER  - 

