AB  - Fragmented healthcare data and strict privacy laws pose challenges in the early detection of diabetes. Federated learning (FL) can be used to collaboratively train prediction models without sharing raw records. But issues such as non-IID data, label skew, and interpretability challenge prevail in real-world set-ups. We customize a unified FL framework for diabetes prediction that (a) combines differential privacy and Paillier-class homomorphic encryption to protect updates, (b) performs dynamic domain adaptation to address distributional differences between the EHRs and continuous glucose/wearable signals, and (c) uses a hybrid Transformer + 1D-CNN local model whereby attention maps provide meaningful clinical explanations. Other components are a fairness audit that rebalances the losses for underrepresented subgroups, secure aggregation of local SHAP/LIME explanations into a global interpretability map, and a global calibration step to smoothen probability reliability. Measured on multi-modal data (simulated splits of MIMIC-IV and OhioT1DM across three clinical sites), this framework achieves an AUC-ROC of 88.3% (near-centralized performance), reduces membership-inference leakage by ~92%, and improves F1 for underrepresented groups by 12.7% over FedAvg/FedProx baselines. The system delivers competitive accuracy, stronger privacy guarantees, and actionable explainability for clinicians.
ER  - 

