AB  - Accelerating wearable Internet-of-Things (IoT) device proliferation toward perpetual health monitoring produces voluminous levels of sensitive physiological data, while creating unprecedented challenges in privacy preservation, communication overhead, and resource limitations. Federated Learning (FL) presents a promising solution enabling model training across devices without sharing raw data; however, deploying FL in wearable IoT is hindered due to minimal energy supplies, unstable connectivity, and susceptibility to inference and poisoning-based attacks. With this work, we introduce a Secure and Energy-Efficient Federated Learning (SE2FL) framework designed especially for wearable IoT applications in continuous health monitoring. $\text{SE}^{2}$ FL blends (i) lightweight homomorphic encryption-based privacy-preserving aggregation, (ii) energy-adaptive client selection considering residual client energy and communication quality, and (iii) a multi-objective optimization approach toward reducing energy expenditure while ensuring model accuracy. Systematic evaluations across benchmark biomedical datasets such as PhysioNet ECG, PAMAP2 activity recognition, and MIMIC-III vital signs indicate $\text{SE}^{2}$ FL reduces energy expenditure by up to 37 %, reduces communication overhead by up to 24 %, and withstands 92 % of data poisoning efforts compared to state-of-the-art FL baselines. Our system guarantees scalable, secure, and sustainable FL-based wearable IoT deployment toward perpetual patient monitoring in real-time intervals, having direct relevance to early disease identification, personalized medicine, and future digital infrastructures supporting healthcare.
ER  - 

