AB  - With the advent of the Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) and the increasing amount of electronic health records (EHR), the need for privacy-preserving, interoperable, and tamper-proof healthcare data sharing architectures in distributed multi-cloud environments has grown more urgent than ever. Current central solutions can be vulnerable to a single point of failure and fail to meet HIPAA, GDPR, and PDPA regulations.This paper suggests the Federated Machine Learning Blockchain (FML-BC) framework, a combination of differentially private federated learning and a permissioned Hyperledger Fabric blockchain that allows for secure, auditable aggregation of models, while avoiding centralization of raw patient information. A Paillier homomorphic encryption layer protects the parameter updates in the process of inter-cloud transmission, and the smart contract-based access control system controls the gradient sharing among different nodes of different hospitals. The system was tested using 12 simulated hospital nodes and two benchmark data sets: MIMIC-III and PhysioNet 2012.The system was tested on MIMIC-III, PhysioNet 2012 and eICU benchmark databases using 12 simulated hospital nodes. Conclusion: FML-BC outperformed state-of-the-art baselines by 4.2% in terms of accuracy, while communication overhead was reduced by 31.5% and the budget for differential privacy $\varepsilon=0.85$. The proposed framework shows high scalability, good data governance and excellent predictive performance, and is therefore a deployable, regulation-compliant solution for cross-institutional healthcare analytics.
